----------------------------------------------------------------------
Credits, inspirations, and special thanks
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Fluxora exists because other projects walked the road first. None of
the projects below are affiliated with Fluxora; the credits are an
acknowledgment that the design and engineering benefited materially
from theirs.

Plex (https://www.plex.tv)
  The original "self-hosted media server" template — what a media
  library + per-device clients + remote streaming should look like
  from a UX standpoint. Fluxora's "Plex meets Syncthing" framing is
  a deliberate reference.

Jellyfin (https://jellyfin.org)
  The open-source proof that a complete media server can be built
  and maintained outside corporate ownership. Fluxora's metadata-
  agent design (Phase-5+ "let users plug in MyAnimeList / TheTVDB
  per library") is directly inspired by Jellyfin's plug-in agents.

Syncthing (https://syncthing.net)
  The "no cloud, peer-to-peer, mDNS-discoverable" philosophy. Fluxora
  borrowed the idea that pairing should be a one-time human-mediated
  trust event, not a perpetual account-and-password.

Tailscale + Cloudflare Tunnel (https://tailscale.com,
                               https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/)
  Made it cheap and reliable to expose a home server to the internet
  without port-forwarding, DDNS, or a static IP. Fluxora's "internet
  streaming via Cloudflare Tunnel" path stands directly on this.

The Movie Database (TMDB, https://www.themoviedb.org)
  Free, community-edited movie + TV metadata, with an API permissive
  enough that small projects like Fluxora can ship metadata + poster
  art at zero cost. We are deeply grateful.

FFmpeg (https://ffmpeg.org)
  The Swiss Army knife of video pipelines. The single most important
  open-source media tool of the last 25 years. Fluxora's stream-copy /
  hardware-encode / HLS-segment / HDR-tonemap pipelines are all just
  thin policy on top of what FFmpeg already does well.

mpv (https://mpv.io)
  The reference open-source video player. Fluxora's clients use mpv
  via media_kit; the playback quality, codec coverage, and HDR support
  are all mpv's, not ours.

Polar (https://polar.sh)
  A small payment platform built for indie devs that doesn't require
  setting up an LLC or wrestling with Stripe Connect. Made the
  paid-tier experiment possible at our scale.

Flutter and the Dart team (https://flutter.dev)
  Cross-platform UI that didn't suck. Without it, Fluxora would have
  been three separate apps in three separate languages.

The Flutter community packages used (see the dependency manifest
above) — every one of them is free volunteer work that this project
depends on.

